--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party dependencies
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Core (always installed):

  pydantic      MIT
  PyYAML        MIT

Optional extras, lazy-imported from `loopsheet.adapters` only. Installing an
extra does not change loopsheet's Apache-2.0 grant, but it does add that
package's license to YOUR deployment:

  paho-mqtt     EPL-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause   [mqtt]
  aiomqtt       BSD-3-Clause              [async]
  asyncua       LGPL-3.0                  [opcua]   <-- copyleft; see below
  pycomm3       MIT                       [enip]
  httpx         BSD-3-Clause              [iotcore]
  pymodbus      BSD-3-Clause              [modbus]
  pint          BSD-3-Clause              [units]
  basyx-python-sdk  MIT                   [aas]

asyncua is LGPL-3.0. It is the single sharpest reason the extras split exists:
no module under `loopsheet.models`, `loopsheet.codec`, `loopsheet.bindings`, or
`loopsheet.catalog` may import it, directly or transitively. Keeping it out of
the core import path keeps the Apache-2.0 grant clean.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Device data
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Catalog files under `src/loopsheet/catalog/data/` describe third-party hardware.
Part numbers, vendor names, and trademarks (ifm, efector, moneo, Rockwell,
Allen-Bradley, EtherNet/IP, IO-Link, OPC UA) belong to their respective owners
and are used here solely for identification and interoperability.

No vendor IODD, EDS, or GSD file is vendored into this repository. Values in
catalog files are transcribed from public datasheets and manuals, with sources
recorded in `docs/research/`. Values that could not be traced to a primary
source ship as `null` marked `# UNVERIFIED`, never as a plausible guess.
